          "low": 2,
          "param": "m"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined_m also produces 1 result/cycle, so it faces the same Fmax >= 1000 MHz problem as pipelined. However, by registering every m stages (m=2..8), the combinational depth per cycle is reduced, which may push Fmax higher than the fully-pipelined case. With m=8 and N=12, each stage does 8 micro-rotations of logic; this is still deep. I search the full m range to let code determine whether any (m, n_iter, data_width) combination can reach >=1000 MHz. If not, this family is also infeasible for throughput. The accuracy constraint (max_abs_err <= 2^-10) is easily met with N>=12 and W>=12, so accuracy is not the binding constraint here; throughput is. I focus n_iter and data_width in the 12-16 range because lower values risk missing the accuracy target while higher values increase area without helping throughput."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Both pipelined and pipelined_m produce 1 result per cycle, so throughput_msps = Fmax/1000. The constraint requires >=1 MSPS, i.e. Fmax >= 1000 MHz. Artix-7 FPGAs typically max out around 200-400 MHz depending on the part and design complexity. Even with aggressive pipelining (m=8), the per-cycle logic depth for 8 CORDIC micro-rotations is substantial and unlikely to reach 1 GHz. Therefore, both families are likely infeasible for the throughput constraint. I allocate 60% to pipelined and 40% to pipelined_m to let code confirm this. The accuracy constraint (max_abs_err <= 2^-10) is easily satisfied with N>=12 and W>=12, so I narrow n_iter and data_width to 12-16 to focus the search on the area/accuracy trade-off within the feasible accuracy region. If both families are confirmed infeasible for throughput, the fleet will need to rely on iterative or unrolled_k (handled by other architects) to meet the spec, since those families produce one result every several cycles and can achieve higher Fmax per cycle, but their throughput will be Fmax/(cycles_per_result), which also needs to be >=1 MSPS. For example, iterative with N=12 needs 15 cycles/result, so Fmax >= 1500 MHz is needed, which is also impossible. unrolled_k with k=8 and N=12 needs ceil(12/8)+3 = 5 cycles/result, so Fmax >= 500 MHz, which is more plausible. This suggests the spec may be infeasible for all families, but that determination is for the fleet to make. My job is to map the front for my assigned families as best as possible."
}

